 "paragraphs": [
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p001.s01",
     "source": "Rangi-potiki had three wives, the first of which was Hine-ahu-papa; from her descended:—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p001"
  },
  {
   "type": "verse",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p002.s01",
     "source": "|  Tu-nuku."
    },
    {
     "id": "p002.s02",
     "source": "Sky    |  Tu-rangi."
    },
    {
     "id": "p002.s03",
     "source": "Powers.  |  Tama-i-koropao."
    },
    {
     "id": "p002.s04",
     "source": "|  Haronga."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p002"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p003.s01",
     "source": "Haronga took to wife Tongo-tongo."
    },
    {
     "id": "p003.s02",
     "source": "Their children were a son and daughter, Te Ra (=The Sun) and Marama (=The Moon)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p003.s03",
     "source": "Haronga perceiving that there was no light for his daughter Marama, gave Te Kohu in marriage to Te Ikaroa , and the Stars were born to give light for the sister of Te Ra, for the child of Tongo-tongo. “ Nga tokorua a Tongo-tongo ” (=the two children of Tongotongo) is a proverbial term for the Sun and Moon at the present day."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p003"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p004.s01",
     "source": "Rangi-potiki’s second wife was Papatuanuku."
    },
    {
     "id": "p004.s02",
     "source": "She gave birth to the following children:—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p004"
  },
  {
   "type": "verse",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p005.s01",
     "source": "Rehua (a star)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s02",
     "source": "Rongo."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s03",
     "source": "Tangaroa."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s04",
     "source": "Tahu."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s05",
     "source": "Punga and Here,   twins."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s06",
     "source": "Hua and Ari,        do."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s07",
     "source": "Nukumera         } twins."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s08",
     "source": "Rango-maraeroa   }"
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s09",
     "source": "Marere-o-tonga   } do."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s10",
     "source": "Takataka-putea   }"
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s11",
     "source": "Tu-matauenga     } do."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s12",
     "source": "Tu-potiki        }"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p005"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p006.s01",
     "source": "Rongo was atua of the kumara ."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p006"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p007.s01",
     "source": "Tangaroa was ancestor of Fish and the Pounamu , which is classed with fish by the Maori ."
    },
    {
     "id": "p007.s02",
     "source": "Tangaroa took to wife Te Anu-matao (=the chilly cold): from which union descended."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p007"
  },
  {
   "type": "verse",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p008.s01",
     "source": "All   |  Te Whata-uira-a-tangaroa."
    },
    {
     "id": "p008.s02",
     "source": "of the  |  Te Whatukura."
    },
    {
     "id": "p008.s03",
     "source": "Fish   |  Poutini."
    },
    {
     "id": "p008.s04",
     "source": "Class.  |  Te Pounamu."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p008"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p009.s01",
     "source": "Tahu was atua presiding over peace and feasts."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p009"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p010.s01",
     "source": "Punga was ancestor of the lizard, shark, and ill-favoured creatures: hence the proverb “ aitanga-a-Punga ” (=child of Punga) to denote an ugly fellow."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p010"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p011.s01",
     "source": "Tu-matauenga was the Maori war God."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p011"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p012.s01",
     "source": "Rangi-potiki ’s third wife was Papa (=Earth)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p012.s02",
     "source": "Tangaroa was accused of having committed adultery with Papa, and Rangipotiki , armed with his spear, went to obtain satisfaction."
    },
    {
     "id": "p012.s03",
     "source": "He found Tangaroa seated by the door of his house, who, when he saw Rangi thus coming towards him, began the following karakia , at the same time striking his right shoulder with his left hand:—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p012"
  },
  {
   "type": "quote",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p013.s01",
     "source": "Tangaroa, Tangaroa, Tangaroa, unravel; Unravel the tangle, Unravel, untwist."
    },
    {
     "id": "p013.s02",
     "source": "Though Rangi is distant, He is to be reached."
    },
    {
     "id": "p013.s03",
     "source": "Some darkness for above, Some light for below Freely give For bright Day"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p013"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p014.s01",
     "source": "This invocation of Tangaroa was scarce ended when Rangi made a thrust at him."
    },
    {
     "id": "p014.s02",
     "source": "Tangaroa warded it off, and it missed him."
    },
    {
     "id": "p014.s03",
     "source": "Then Tangaroa made a thrust at Rangi, and pierced him quite through the thigh, and he fell."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p014"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p015.s01",
     "source": "While Rangi lay wounded he begat his child Kueo (=Moist)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s02",
     "source": "The cause of this name was Rangi’s wetting his couch while he lay ill of his wound."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s03",
     "source": "After Kueo, he begat Mimi-ahi, so-called from his making water by the fireside."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s04",
     "source": "Next he begat Tane-tuturi (=straight-leg-Tane), so-called because Rangi could now stretch his legs."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s05",
     "source": "Afterwards he begat Tane-pepeki (=bent-leg-Tane), so-called because Rangi could sit with his knees bent."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s06",
     "source": "The next child was Tane-ua-tika ( =straight-neck-Tane ), for Rangi’s neck was now straight, and he could hold up his head."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s07",
     "source": "The next child born was called Tane-ua-ha (=strong-neck-Tane), for Rangi’s neck was strong."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s08",
     "source": "Then was born Tane-te-waiora (=lively Tane), so called because Rangi was quite recovered."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s09",
     "source": "Then was born Tane-nui-a-Rangi (=Tane great son of Rangi)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s10",
     "source": "And last of all was born Paea, a daughter."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s11",
     "source": "She was the last of Rangi’s children."
    },
    {
     "id": "p015.s12",
     "source": "With Paea they came to an end, so she was named Paea, which signifies ‘closed.’"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p015"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p016.s01",
     "source": "Some time after the birth of these children the thought came to Tane-nui-a-Rangi to separate their father from them."
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s02",
     "source": "Tane had seen the light of the Sun shining under the armpit of Rangi; so he consulted with his elder brothers what they should do."
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s03",
     "source": "They all said, “Let us kill our father, because he has shut us up in darkness, and let us leave our mother for our parent.”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s04",
     "source": "But Tane advised, “Do not let us kill our father, but rather let us raise him up above, so that there may be light.”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s05",
     "source": "To this they consented; so they prepared ropes, and when Rangi was sound asleep they rolled him over on the ropes, and Paea took him on her back."
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s06",
     "source": "Two props were also placed under Rangi."
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s07",
     "source": "The names of the props were Tokohurunuku, and Tokohururangi."
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s08",
     "source": "Then lifting him with the aid of these two props, they shoved him upwards."
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s09",
     "source": "Then Papa thus uttered her farewell to Rangi."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p016"
  },
