   ],
   "id": "p215"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p216.s01",
     "source": "."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p216"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p217.s01",
     "source": "”"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p217"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p218.s01",
     "source": "So Rangi dwelt above, and Tane and his brothers dwelt below with their mother, Papa."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p218"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p219.s01",
     "source": "Some time after this Tane desired to have his mother Papa for his wife."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s02",
     "source": "But Papa said, “Do not turn your inclination towards me, for evil will come to you."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s03",
     "source": "Go to your ancestor Mumuhango.”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s04",
     "source": "So Tane took Mumuhango to wife, who brought forth the totara tree."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s05",
     "source": "Tane returned to his mother dissatisfied, and his mother said, “Go to your ancestor Hine-tu-a-maunga (=the mountain maid).”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s06",
     "source": "So Tane took Hine-tu-a-maunga to wife, who conceived, but did not bring forth a child."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s07",
     "source": "Her offspring was the rusty water of mountains, and the monster reptiles common to mountains."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s08",
     "source": "Tane was displeased, and returned to his mother."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s09",
     "source": "Papa said to him “Go to your ancestor Rangahore.”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s10",
     "source": "So Tane went, and took that female for a wife, who brought forth stone."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s11",
     "source": "This greatly displeased Tane, who again went back to Papa."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s12",
     "source": "Then Papa said “Go to your ancestor Ngaore (=the tender one).”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s13",
     "source": "Tane took Ngaore to wife."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s14",
     "source": "And Ngaore gave birth to the toetoe (a species of rush-like grass)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s15",
     "source": "Tane returned to his mother in displeasure."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s16",
     "source": "She next advised him, “Go to your ancestor Pakoti.”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s17",
     "source": "Tane did as he was bid, but Pakoti only brought forth harakeke (=phormium tenax)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s18",
     "source": "Tane had a great many other wives at his mother’s bidding, but none of them pleased him, and his heart was greatly troubled, because no child was born to give birth to Man; so he thus addressed his mother—“Old lady, there will never be any progeny for me.”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s19",
     "source": "Thereupon Papa said, “Go to your ancestor, Ocean, who is grumbling there in the distance."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s20",
     "source": "When you reach the beach at Kura-waka, gather up the earth in the form of man.”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s21",
     "source": "So Tane went and scraped up the earth at Kura-waka."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s22",
     "source": "He gathered up the earth, the body was formed, and then the head, and the arms; then he joined on the legs, and patted down the surface of the belly, so as to give the form of man; and when he had done this, he returned to his mother and said, “The whole body of the man is finished.”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s23",
     "source": "Thereupon his mother said, “Go to your ancestor Mauhi, she will give the raho ."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s24",
     "source": "Go to your ancestor Whete, she will give the timutimu ."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s25",
     "source": "Go to your ancestor Taua-ki-te-marangai, she will give the paraheka ."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s26",
     "source": "Go to your ancestor Pungaheko, she has the huruhuru .”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s27",
     "source": "So Tane went to these female ancestors, who gave him the things asked for."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s28",
     "source": "He then went to Kura-waka."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s29",
     "source": "Katahi ka whakanoho ia i nga raho ki roto i nga kuwha o te wahine i hanga ki te one: Ka mau era."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s30",
     "source": "Muri atu ka whakanoho ia ko te timutimu na Whete i homai ki waenga i nga raho; muri atu ko te paraheka na Taua-ki-te-marangai i homai ka whakanoho ki te take o te timutimu: muri iho ko te huruhuru na Pungaheko i homai ka whakanoho ki runga i te puke."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s31",
     "source": "Ka oti, katahi ka tapa ko Hineahuone."
    },
    {
     "id": "p219.s32",
     "source": "Then he named this female form Hine-ahu-one (=The earth formed maid)."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p219"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p220.s01",
     "source": "Tane took Hine-ahu-one to wife."
    },
    {
     "id": "p220.s02",
     "source": "She first gave birth to Tiki-tohua—the egg of a bird from which have sprung all the birds of the air."
    },
    {
     "id": "p220.s03",
     "source": "After that, Tiki-kapakapa was born—a female."
    },
    {
     "id": "p220.s04",
     "source": "Then first was born for Tane a human child."
    },
    {
     "id": "p220.s05",
     "source": "Tane took great care of Tiki-kapakapa, and when she grew up he gave her a new name, Hine-a-tauira (=the pattern maid)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p220.s06",
     "source": "Then he took her to wife, and she bore a female child who was named Hine-titamauri."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p220"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p221.s01",
     "source": "One day Hine-a-tauira said to Tane, “Who is my father?”"
    },
    {
     "id": "p221.s02",
     "source": "Tane laughed."
    },
    {
     "id": "p221.s03",
     "source": "A second time Hine-a-tauira asked the same question."
    },
    {
     "id": "p221.s04",
     "source": "Then Tane made a sign: and the woman understood, and her heart was dark, and she gave herself up to mourning, and fled away to Rikiriki, and to Naonao, to Rekoreko, to Waewae-te-Po, and to Po."
    },
    {
     "id": "p221.s05",
     "source": "The woman fled away, hanging down her head."
    },
    {
     "id": "p221.s06",
     "source": "Then she took the name of Hine-nui-te-Po (=great woman of Night)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p221.s07",
     "source": "Her farewell words to Tane were—“Remain, O Tane, to pull up our offspring to Day; while I go below to drag down our offspring to Night.”"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p221"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p222.s01",
     "source": "Tane sorrowed for his daughter-wife, and cherished his daughter Hinetitamauri; and when she grew up he gave her to Tiki to be his wife, and their first-born child was Tiki-te-pou-mua."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p222"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p223.s01",
     "source": "The following narrative is a continuation of the history of Hinenuitepo from another source:—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p223"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p224.s01",
     "source": "After Hinenuitepo fled away to her ancestors in the realms of Night, she gave birth to Te Po-uriuri (=The Dark one), and to Te Po-tangotango (=The very dark), and afterwards to Pare-koritawa, who married Tawaki, one of the race of Rangi."
    },
    {
     "id": "p224.s02",
     "source": "Hence the proverb when the sky is seen covered with small clouds “ Parekoritawa is tilling her garden. ” When Tawaki climbed to Heaven with Parekoritawa, he repeated this karakia :—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p224"
  },
  {
   "type": "quote",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p225.s01",
     "source": "Ascend, O Tawaki, by the narrow path, By which the path of Rangi was followed; The path of Tu-kai-te-uru."
    },
    {
     "id": "p225.s02",
     "source": "The narrow path is climbed, The broad path is climbed, The path by which was followed Your ancestors, Te Aonui, Te Ao-roa, Te Ao-whititera."
    },
    {
     "id": "p225.s03",
     "source": "Now you mount up To your Ihi , To your Mana , To the Thousands above, To your Ariki , To your Tapairu , To your Pukenga , To your Wananga , To your Tauira ."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p225"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p226.s01",
     "source": "When Tawaki and Parekoritawa mounted to the Sky, they left behind them a token—a black moth—a token of the mortal body."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p226"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p227.s01",
     "source": "Pare gave birth to Uenuku (=Rainbow)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p227.s02",
     "source": "Afterwards she brought forth Whatitiri (=Thunder)."
    },
    {
     "id": "p227.s03",
     "source": "Hence the rainbow in the sky, and the thunder-clap."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p227"
  }
 ]
}